(V)--one of the four lakes in Meru; surrounds all the sides of the Meru over which is established the capital of the लोकपालस्; at the foot of the वैद्यूत hill from which the R. Sarayu rises;
1: M. ११३. ४६; १२१. १६; १६३. ८६; १८३. 2; १९४. 8; Br. II. १८. १५; २१. २९-33; वा. ४७. १४; ५०. ८७-90; १११. 4.
north of the Ganges;
2: Br. I. 1. ७६; M. ७०. २०; १०७. 2; वा. ३६. १६; ४२. २७; ७७. ११०-11.
a तीर्थ sacred to Kumuda and the पितृस्;
3: M. १३. २७; २२. २३.
fit for श्राद्ध; contains a shrine on its banks;
4: Br. III. १३, ५८, ११५-6; IV. 2. २५-6.
in इलावृत.
5: Vi. II. 2. २६.
Kardama went there with his wife;
6: भा. I. 5. १०; III. २३. ४०.
a reference to two हम्सस् of the lake; one of them who took to household life was पुरञ्जन; hearing from the other हम्स he remem- bered his old place and became calm;
7: Ib. V. २८. ५४ and ६३.
Indra spent 1,००० years in the lotus stalks of the lake;
8: Ib. VI. १३. १४-5.
here was the third rebirth of कौशिक's seven sons as चक्रवाक birds and their final salvation after becoming Siddhas.
9: M. २०. १७; २१. ३५.
ऊर्वशी sported with पुरूरवस् in.
१०: Vi. IV. 6. ४८.

(XIV)--(वैवस्वत)--the seventh Manu, also known as ‘श्राद्धदेव’.
1: भा. VIII. १३. 1-9; Br. II. ३६. 4, ८१; III. ५९. २२ and ३८; ६३. २१५; IV. 1. 6-२८; वा. ८४. २२; Vi. IV. 1. 6-7.
Son of सम्ज्ञा and Vivasvat (सुरेणु and विवस्वान् वा। प्।): wife श्रद्धा: father of ten sons of whom इक्ष्वाकु was the eldest;
2: Br. II. ३८. 1.
in this epoch Puran- dara was Indra: कश्यप, Atri and others were sages: आदित्- yas and Vasus were gods: वामन was the manifestation of Hari;
3: भा. VIII. १३. 1-9.
a क्षत्रिय मन्त्रवादिन्: His sacrifice was disturbed by sons of वरूत्रि who were burnt by Indra in the वेदि: [page२-621+ ४१] a प्रजापति: king and दण्डधर.
4: Br. II. ३२. १२०; ३८. २६ and ३२; III. 1. 3 and 6; 8. २१; १०. ९८; ६०. 7; M. १४५. ११५; २४८. १५.
Saved by the Matsya Hari during the deluge. After anointing his son on the throne, M. performed penance on the Malaya Mt. for a lac of years when ब्रह्मा blessed him as protector of the universe after the प्रलय। Once in making a water oblation to his manes, a fish fell into his hand which he put into his water vessel. Finding its rapid growth he placed it in a well, tank, Ganges and the sea respectively. From its abnormal growth he suspected it to be an asura or वासुदेव; on questioning it he found it to be the Lord and was asked to use it (the fish) as the life boat when the whole world went down in the deluge, saving at the same time some lives;
5: Ib. 1. ११ to the end; 2. १६; 9. 1; १६. 1; ५२. 3.
performed तपस् on the यमुना for one hundred years with a view to get progeny;
6: भा. I. 3. १५; VIII. २४. ५०; IX. 1. 3-१२; 2. 1; 6. 4.
see Satyavrata; celebrated an अश्वमेध in honour of Mitra and वरुण; out came इला who went to them;
7: Br. III. ६०. 1-१०.
the Lord of men, and seven worlds with towns;
8: वा. ७०. १८.
divided the Vedas into four for the pro- gress of the world;
9: Ib. ६०. 8.
in order to get sons he performed the मित्रावरुण ritual; owing to the होत'स् carelessness a daughter इला came out; she became again a male Sudyumna by name; by an imprecation of शिव he again became a woman near Budha's hermitage. Budha got by her a son by name पुरूरवस्;
१०: Vi. IV. 1. 8-१२.
interpretation of शब्दब्रह्म by.
११: Ib. VI. 5. ६४.

(I)--(also मन्त्रस्थानम्, Council Cham- ber) consultation to be held with many and individually and with people in whom the king has confidence; at least one expert must be consulted; generally with people learned in the three Vedas, elders who have the ability to correct the erring king;
1: M. २१५. ४८-52.
kingdom rests on mantra;
2: Ib. २२०. ३३.
king not to decide alone nor to consult too many;
3: Ib. २२०. ३७.
भण्ड's War Council.
4: Br. IV. २१. २१; २५. 3

--take the food offered in sacrifices to the पितृस् as the calf finds out its mother among the lost cows;
1: Br. II. २८. ९१; वा. ५९. ६१.
came out of discontent, fear, difficulties, happiness and misery of ऋषिस्; later put into form and order by sages; differences in मन्त्रस् as many as twenty-four;
2: Br. II. ३२. ६८; ३३. ४२; वा. ५९. ३५-61; M. १४५. ६२-3.
the two major divisions --ग्राम्यम् and आरण्यकम्;
3: Br. II. ३५. ७३, ८५; IV. 8. ५१-3, ५७.
Veda mantras, विष्णु mantra, दुर्गा mantra, गाणपत mantra, etc.;
4: Ib. IV. ३८. 4.
ety of.
5: वा. ५९. १४१.

(II)--the chief counsellor of the king;
1: वा. ५७. ७०.
looks after the kingdom in the absence of the king;
2: M. ११५. १७; २१७. १८.
to be consulted before undertaking an expedition;
3: Ib. २२३. 9; २४०. २७.
residence of.
4: Ib. २५४. २०.

(II)--a Mt. of कुशद्वीप, from waters called मन्दस्;
1: Br. II. १३. ३६; १९. ५६; वा. ३६. १९; ४२. १४; ४५. ९०; ४९. ५१; १०१. २८८.
a son of Meru;
2: Ib. ३०. ३३.
on the other side of गन्धमा- dhana; its महावृक्ष is केतुराट्.
3: Ib. ३५. १६.

(II)--a R. sacred to the पितृस्;
1: M. २२. २३.
rises from ऋष्यवान्;
2: Ib. १०५. १०; ११४. २५.
water for bathing a deity.
3: Ib. २६७. २०.

--periods of Manus; seven in number and seven to come, of स्वायम्भुव, स्वारोचिष, Auttama, तामस, Raivata, चाक्षुष, सावर्णि (after Vaivasvata in म्। प्।), रौच्य (ब्र्। प्।); Vaivasvata present according to the म्। प्।) Ruci, Bhautya, मेरुसावर्णि, Rta, ऋतधामान् and विष्वक्सेन; the last seven are future Manus;
1: Br. II. 6. 6, २०; ३६. 1-4; M. 2. २२; 9. 2-३६; वा. २१. ११, १४, १९, ३८, ४४; ५७. ३३-6; Vi. I. 3. १८.
one of the five characteristics of the पुराण;
2: M. ५३. ६५; Br. I. 1. ३७; वा. 4. १०.
calculation of the duration of the epochs of Manus;
3: M. १४२. ३०.
constitutes ७१ yugas at the end of which comes क्षय
4: वा. १०. १२; ३२. ४१.
according to मानुष and Divyavat- sara.
5: Ib. ६१. १३८-144; १५०. १७६.

--a son of युवनाश्व, was born of him by splitting open the right side of his stomach; to the crying child Indra gave his first finger to suckle and called him Trasadasyu (s.v.) as he was a terror to the Dasyus; celebrated as a great emperor by the पौराणिकस्, having lust for more territory; married (Bindu- मती-भा and वि। प्।) चैत्ररथी, daughter of शशबिन्दु, (Satabindu-वि। प्।); had three sons, Purukusta, अम्बरीष, and Mucukunda besides fifty daughters, all of whom were married to Saubhari;
1: भा. IX. 6. ३०-38; 7. 1; X. ५१. १४; XII. 3. 9; वा. ९९. १३०; Vi. IV. 2. ६१-112; Br. III. ६३. ६८-72; M. १२. ३४; ४९. 8.
he built for them crystal palaces; gave them all comforts and himself lived constantly with each of them by his yogic powers; they gave birth to १५० sons and मान्धात visited them after a long time and found them happy; was blessed by the sage and returned home; a क्षत्रोपेतद्विज;
2: Br. III. ६६. ८६.
an incarnation of विष्णु as human being with Uthathya, in the १५थ् Treta (with Uttankapurohita in the १५थ् Treta1yuga-म्। प्।);
3: Ib. III. ७३. ९०; M. ४७. २४३; वा. ९८. ९०.
an Angirasa and मन्त्रकृत्;
4: Br. II. ३२. १०८; M. १४५. १०२; वा. ५९. ९९.
wor- shipped Hari as यज्ञ, knew the yoga power of Hari and got over his माया;
5: भा. II. 7. ४४.
a great conquering monarch; a popular king, a यज्व, of good character;
6: वा. ८८. ६६-70.
became a Brahmana;
7: Ib. ९१. ११५.
Tattya was his purohita.
8: Ib. ९९. १३०.
